> 
> The trick is to enchant your armour to +3 and then read a b?EA.This
> will make the armour +4 or +5 (I think there is a possibility of +6
> but I could be wrong). 

You are ;-).  If the armor is +0 - +2, you can get up to +3 
enchantment.  For +3 - +5, you get +1 or +2.  However, it is 
possible for the armor to survive enchantment beyond the safe 
+3 limit; the chance is small, however (1/current enchantment).  
If you have, say, three cloaks of MR, all +5, you might go for it 
and try for two of them to get +6 or even +7, but that would 
certainly be only for style points, since there is only 
a very small difference between AC -42 and AC -44.  

> If it goes to +4, enchant up the rest of your
> armour.If you _still_ have spare scrolls either use a disenchanter or
> a c?EA to bring the piece of armour back down to +3.
> I often trap a disenchanter in a room in the castle for this purpose.
> 
> With most weapons you can engrave a few letters to bring the weapon
> down 1 enchantment (except for blunt weapons).
> 
> Thus you need never blow up your stuff by over-enchanting unless you
> have so many spare scrolls that you are trying for +6 or +7 armour (I
> once heard of a +10 Excalibur).

Artifacts have a higher enchantment tolerance (+5 is save), 
but still +10 would need at least 3 non--save enchantments 
(7->8->9->10); the probability of success is below 0.2%, if 
I calculated correctly.  It's an almost sure blow-up to try 
this, and it's definitely only worth trying if you're 
looking for style points.  

